WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:02.949
One last photograph.

00:00:02.949 --> 00:00:07.990
Many coal miners remained true to traditions,
even as the decades saw dramatic changes in

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:08.990
the industry.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:13.540
At a miners' association event in November,
they said a final farewell to the community

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.699
where the industry has always played a central
role.

00:00:16.699 --> 00:00:19.240
It was a moving moment.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:24.250
"It really hits you hard to realize that it's
all coming to an end.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:29.199
A lot of the guys have tears in their eyes
right now."

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:35.200
In the heyday of coal mining, there were 163
pits in Germany.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.399
Pit manager Jürgen Kröker closed two of
them.

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:41.710
Prosper-Haniel is his third and last.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:46.520
"The other day when I was down the shaft I
thought, 'This is probably the last time you'll

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:50.570
be down here with the crew, the last time
you'll come down to the coal face.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:54.770
The world has really moved on."

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:58.840
Miners will continue to descend the 12 hundred
meters into the earth's bowels here until

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.520
the end of next year.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.510
But instead of coal, they'll be bringing up
machines, cables and old iron.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.910
Most of the equipment will end up in Turkey
or China.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:13.050
Many of the miners are bitter that their machines
are being sold off to competitors who will

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.720
end up supplying Germany with cheap imported
coal.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.350
Michael Gerdes is the only miner in the German
parliament, the Bundestag.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:25.900
He worked in the Bottrop mine for decades
... and believes Germany's move to end the

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:30.940
industry is a big mistake.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.100
We're going to miss German black coal one
day.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.190
The fact that we're no longer producing it
in Germany doesn't mean coal-fired power generation

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:38.400
will cease.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.790
So we'll need black coal from other countries,
and their prices will clearly rise.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:47.190
And the standards that we introduced here
don't exist in those countries.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:52.290
The landscape is still marked by winding towers
and coking plants, but other companies also

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.440
moved in here long ago.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.650
Bottrop is betting that by developing something
new on the many areas once dominated by mining,

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:03.510
it'll be able to secure jobs for the future.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:09.000
I'm optimistic, because we have good projects
to further develop areas.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.450
First we'll clean up contaminated old mining
sites, then we'll help new companies set up

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:14.450
here.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:18.720
It's a big project."

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.270
Europe's largest Chamber of Horrors has set
up shop in the old pit's historically listed

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:24.270
laundromat.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.200
A positive investment in the region in the
post-coal age.

